there are big developments in washington  on a possible new package for gun  regulations as edelke reports president  biden and majority of senators are  behind the bill that finally emerged  from bipartisan negotiations last night  the motion is agreed to  overnight the senate took its first  steps towards passing major gun related  legislation in an attempt to address  recent mass shootings in new york texas  and california the bill is a bipartisan  deal that would expand gun background  checks for people under 21 allowing up  to 10 business days to review juvenile  and mental health records it provides  federal money for states enacting new  red flag laws designed to temporarily  seize guns from people deemed dangerous  and it closes the so-called boyfriend  loophole preventing partners convicted  of domestic abuse who live outside a  victim's home from buying a gun we have  a chance to break this  30-year period of silence it is a  compromise  it is a bipartisan  compromise there's also hundreds of  millions of dollars more for mental  health and safety resources in schools  plus tough new penalties for those that  buy and then turn around and sell a  weapon anyone barred from owning guns i  want to make sure we actually do  something useful  something that is  capable of becoming a law the national  rifle association opposes the agreement  saying it does little to truly address  violent crime and opens the door to  unnecessary burdens on the exercise of  second amendment freedom but that  doesn't seem to bother negotiators eager  to show congress can do something  anything to address gun crimes it's a  good start and i think it's going to  give us a lot of help for children  as we mentioned president biden is  supportive of this plan senate leaders  anticipate it can be passed by the end  of this week and house speaker nancy  pelosi says she hopes to hold a vote on  it as soon as possible nate  and it's a busy day at the white house  and speaking of biden what can you tell  us about president biden's latest action  to get gas prices down yeah interesting  move this morning here at the white  house the president announcing he's  calling on congress to suspend the  federal gas tax which is 18 cents for a  gallon of regular for three months so  conceivably if they pass to july one  they would run through the end of  september while the average price of gas  has come down below five dollars a  gallon the white house says this is  designed to do everything possible to  reduce costs for families especially as  the busy holiday travel season begins  nate  ed thank you
 

